  "form_markdown": "# mail: reading a message

The form for reading, drawn from research 01a19b3f (the reading toy) and 01a18fb8 (the parser
toy) under intent 01a1132e. Requirements and issues are written from it; the toys' code is
evidence, not the product. The writer's form (01a18fda) governs everything this form does not
restate: its Craft, Teaching and Tests sections hold here.

## The four ideas

1. **Reading reports; it never mends.** A received message is whatever its sender wrote. Mail
   reads what can be read, records a problem for each part that cannot, and never supplies a
   value in its place: no invented address, no "now" for a missing date.
2. **Structure is byte ranges, not decoded text.** A message is a tree of parts, each a header
   and a body located by offsets into the stored bytes. Nothing is decoded to build it. IMAP
   serves these ranges as they are; text is decoded only when someone asks for it.
3. **One lexer under every structured field.** Addresses, message identifiers and dates are
   words, quoted strings and literals with comments and folding between them (RFC 5322 3.2).
   One lexer drops what carries no meaning; each reader is a small grammar over its tokens.
4. **The obsolete forms are read.** RFC 5322 section 4 forbids writing them and requires
   reading them: routes, empty list elements, spaced dots, two-digit years, named zones.
   The writer never writes them; the reader always accepts them.

## Closures

Reading has no impure input, so it is functions, not closures:

```ts
const read = readMessage(bytes);       // headers, structure, problems
const text = partText(binary(bytes), part);  // one part, decoded, when asked
```

## Where the code lives

Current choice (2026-11-01), revisable. `@rafters/mail` gains the subpath `./read`; the
version stays 0.3.0 (one minor changeset, `.changeset/reading.md`). No new dependency.

Files, in reading order, with what each exports. A folder's `index.ts` is what its subpath
publishes; the others are internal.

| File | The concept | Rule it cites | Exports |
|---|---|---|---|
| `packages/core/src/read/lexer.ts` | the words of a structured field | RFC 5322 3.2 | `Token`, `lex` |
| `packages/core/src/read/fields.ts` | header fields in order, unfolded | RFC 5322 2.2.3 | `Field`, `headerFields` |
| `packages/core/src/read/address.ts` | mailboxes and groups, obsolete forms included | RFC 5322 4.4 | `ReadMailbox`, `ReadGroup`, `ReadAddress`, `readAddresses`, `sameMailbox` |
| `packages/core/src/read/message-id.ts` | message identifiers | RFC 5322 3.6.4 | `readMessageIds` |
| `packages/core/src/read/date.ts` | a date that is a real moment | RFC 5322 3.3 | `ReadDate`, `readDate` |
| `packages/core/src/read/mime.ts` | the tree of parts as byte ranges | RFC 2046 5.1.1 | `Range`, `Part`, `parseParts`, `section`, `contentType`, `partFields`, `bodyLines`, `MAX_DEPTH` |
| `packages/core/src/read/decode.ts` | a part's text, and encoded words | RFC 2045 6.1 | `transferDecode`, `partText`, `decodeWords` |
| `packages/core/src/read/index.ts` | a received message read | RFC 5322 3.6 | `readMessage`, `binary`, `Read`, `Problem`, `READ_PROBLEMS`, and re-exports of every exported name above |

`read` imports nothing from the rest of core, and nothing in core imports `read`. Because it
cannot import `encoding/never.ts`, `read` has no `switch` or `if` chain over a union: a token's
kind is resolved by a ternary whose last arm is the shared case (an atom and a special are both
written as their value), and `"group" in a` is one test with two outcomes. Each file
opens with its citation and a quoted sentence of the rule (the writer's Teaching section);
`tests/teaching.test.ts` gains the eight rows.

**Workspace edits:** `tsconfig.base.json` path `"@rafters/mail/read"` to
`./packages/core/src/read/index.ts`; core `tsup.config.ts` entry and `package.json` `exports`
gain `./read`. `packages/core/tests/criteria.json` gains every criterion id of FR-MAIL-002 to
