## Schemas and types

Reading takes bytes, not input to validate, so its types are written by hand (the writer's Craft
exception for shapes mail produces rather than parses). Every field that could not be read is
`null`, never a guess.

```ts
export const READ_PROBLEMS = [
  "bare-line-break", // a CR or LF not in a CRLF pair (RFC 5322 2.2)
  "line-too-long", // a header line over 998 characters (RFC 5322 2.1.1)
  "no-colon", // a header line that is neither a field nor a continuation
  "repeated-field", // a field RFC 5322 3.6 allows once, found more than once
  "missing-required", // no Date or no From (RFC 5322 3.6)
  "unparsed-value", // an address, message-id or date field that fits no grammar
  "invalid-date", // a date that parses but is not a real moment
  "multipart-no-boundary", // a multipart Content-Type with no boundary parameter
  "multipart-unclosed", // a multipart with no closing boundary line
  "too-deep", // multiparts and messages nested deeper than MAX_DEPTH
] as const;
export type Problem = { code: (typeof READ_PROBLEMS)[number]; field: string | null; detail: string };
// field and detail, exactly:
//   bare-line-break        null            "a CR or LF outside a CRLF pair"
//   line-too-long          null            "a line of N characters" (the first such line)
//   no-colon               null            the line's first 40 characters
//   repeated-field         the field name  "N times"
//   missing-required       the field name  "absent"
//   unparsed-value         the field name  its unfolded value's first 80 characters; for date, "not a date"
//   invalid-date           "date"          the date's problem text
//   multipart-no-boundary  "content-type"  the part's type
//   multipart-unclosed     "content-type"  the boundary
//   too-deep               "content-type"  "nesting over 50"

export type Token = { kind: "atom" | "quoted" | "literal" | "special"; value: string; spaced: boolean };
export type Field = { name: string; value: string }; // name as written, value unfolded and trimmed

export type ReadMailbox = { name: string | null; local: string; domain: string };
export type ReadGroup = { group: string; members: ReadMailbox[] };
export type ReadAddress = ReadMailbox | ReadGroup;

export type ReadDate =
  | { instant: Date; zone: string | null } // zone null: -0000, military or unknown name
  | { instant: null; problem: string };

export type Range = { start: number; end: number }; // [start, end) into the stored bytes
export type Part = {
  header: Range;
  body: Range;
  type: string; // lowercase type/subtype
  params: Record<string, string>; // lowercase names, values unquoted
  children: Part[];
  message: Part | null; // the encapsulated message of a message/rfc822 part
};

export type Read = {
  fields: Field[]; // every header field, in order, repeats kept
  from: ReadMailbox[] | null;
  sender: ReadMailbox | null;
  replyTo: ReadAddress[] | null;
  to: ReadAddress[] | null;
  cc: ReadAddress[] | null;
  bcc: ReadAddress[] | null;
  subject: string | null; // as written: encoded words are not decoded here
  date: ReadDate | null; // null when there is no Date field
  messageId: string | null;
  inReplyTo: string[] | null;
  references: string[] | null;
  root: Part;
  problems: Problem[];
};

export function lex(text: string): { tokens: Token[]; problem: string | null };
export function headerFields(text: string): { fields: Field[]; problems: Problem[] };
export function readAddresses(text: string): ReadAddress[] | null;
export function sameMailbox(a: ReadMailbox, b: ReadMailbox): boolean;
export function readMessageIds(text: string): string[] | null;
export function readDate(text: string): ReadDate;
export function parseParts(text: string): { root: Part; problems: Problem[] };
export function section(root: Part, path: string): Part | null;
export const MAX_DEPTH = 50;
export function contentType(value: string): { type: string; params: Record<string, string> };
export function partFields(text: string, part: Part): Field[];
export function bodyLines(text: string, part: Part): number;
export function transferDecode(text: string, encoding: string): Uint8Array;
export function partText(text: string, part: Part): string;
export function decodeWords(value: string): string;
export function binary(bytes: Uint8Array): string;
export function readMessage(bytes: Uint8Array): Read;
```

`text` arguments are the message as a binary string: one character per byte, each byte's value
its char code, built with `String.fromCharCode` over chunks of 8,192 bytes. Offsets into it are
byte offsets. Received mail may be 8-bit; nothing is decoded to build this string.

## Rules

**Lexer** (RFC 5322 3.2). White space (space, tab, CR, LF) and comments are dropped and set
`spaced` on the next token. A comment starts at `(` and ends when every `(` inside it is closed;
`\` quotes the next character inside it. A quoted string runs from `"` to the next unquoted
`"`; a domain literal from `[` to `]`; inside both, `\` is removed and the next character kept,
and CR and LF are dropped. An atom is a run of `atext` (RFC 5322 3.2.3) or any character from
U+0080 (RFC 6532 3.2). Every other character is a `special` token of one character. An unclosed
comment, quoted string or literal ends lexing with the tokens so far and its problem text
(`unclosed comment`, `unclosed quoted string`, `unclosed domain literal`).

**Header fields** (RFC 5322 2.2, 2.2.3). The header is the text before the first empty line.
`headerFields` records `bare-line-break` once when the text holds a CR not followed by LF or an
LF not preceded by CR, then splits lines on CRLF, CR or LF. A line over 998 characters records
`line-too-long` once. A line starting with space or tab continues the previous field: the line
is appended as it is, so unfolding removes only the line break. A line with a colon starts a
field: the name is the text before the first colon, trimmed; the value the rest. An empty line
is skipped. Any other line records `no-colon` and reading continues. A continuation line with
no field before it is read as a line of its own. `bare-line-break` is recorded first;
`line-too-long` and `no-colon` follow in line order; then `repeated-field` in the order of the
names below, then `missing-required` for `date`, then `from`.
Values are trimmed after unfolding. Each of `date`, `from`, `sender`, `reply-to`, `to`, `cc`,
`bcc`, `message-id`, `in-reply-to`, `references` and `subject` (compared without case) found
more than once records `repeated-field` with that name. No `date` or no `from` records
`missing-required` with that name.

**Addresses** (RFC 5322 3.4, 4.4). Over the lexer's tokens:

- A *phrase* is one or more atoms, quoted strings or `.` specials; its text joins their values
  with one space before each token after the first that is `spaced`, none otherwise
  (`Joe Q. Public`).
- A *dotted* name is an atom or quoted string, then any number of `.` and another; for a
  domain, an atom-and-dots name or one literal (`[...]`, kept with its brackets). A quoted
  word keeps its quotes in the result.
- A *mailbox* is an optional phrase, `<`, an optional route (`@`, everything to the next `:`,
  dropped), an addr-spec and `>`; with no phrase the name is `null`. Or it is an addr-spec
  alone, name `null`.
- An *addr-spec* is a dotted local part, `@`, a dotted domain.
- A *group* is a phrase, `:`, mailboxes each followed by `,` or `;` (empty elements skipped),
  `;`.
- A *list* is addresses separated by commas; empty elements are skipped. An empty field value
  is an empty list, with no problem, except in From and Sender (below).

`readAddresses` returns `null` when lexing fails or any element fits none of these, and the
caller records `unparsed-value` for the field. From is read as a list of mailboxes and Sender
as exactly one (RFC 5322 3.6.2): a group in either, an empty list, or more than one Sender
makes the field `null` and `unparsed-value`. Local parts and domains are returned as written.
`sameMailbox` compares header addresses as RFC 5322 3.4.1 does and as the writer's `sameAddress` does: domains ignoring ASCII case only (`A`-`Z`), local parts exactly
(`canonicalLocal` of form 01a19a5c is for matching mail's own directory, never for reading).

**Message identifiers** (RFC 5322 3.6.4, 4.5.4). Every `<` begins an identifier, which is the
concatenation of the token values up to the next `>` (quoted words keep their quotes, literals
their brackets). Tokens outside brackets are skipped (obsolete References may hold phrases).
An identifier with no closing `>`, or that is not exactly one `@` with text on both sides,
makes the field `null` and `unparsed-value`. Identifiers keep their order. A field whose lexing fails is `null` and `unparsed-value`.

**Dates** (RFC 5322 3.3, 4.3). The tokens other than `,` and `:` are the words, as written; names
(day, month, zone) compare without case. A first word that is a day name (`sun` to `sat`) is the
weekday and is removed. The rest are
day, month (`jan` to `dec`), year, hour, minute, then either second and zone, or zone alone
(second `00`). Day is one or two digits; year two to four; hour, minute and second two each;
otherwise `{ instant: null, problem: "not a date" }`. A two-digit year under 50 is 2000 plus,
otherwise 1900 plus; a three-digit year is 1900 plus (RFC 5322 4.3). A zone is `+hhmm` or
`-hhmm` (minutes over 59: problem `zone minutes over 59`); a zone that begins with `+` or `-`
and is not that is `not a date`. Otherwise it is a name: `UT` and `GMT` are 0,
`EST` -5, `EDT` -4, `CST` -6, `CDT` -5, `MST` -7, `MDT` -6, `PST` -8, `PDT` -7 hours. `-0000`,
military letters and any other name are unknown: the instant is computed as Universal Time and
`zone` is `null`. A known zone is returned as written (`GMT`, `Gmt`, `-0600`). Hour over 23, minute over 59 or second over 60 is `not a time of day`. A day
the month does not have is `not a day of that month`; a weekday that does not match is `day of
the week does not match`. Problems are checked in this order: not a date, zone minutes, time of
day, day of the month, weekday. The day is checked on the date alone (built with
`setUTCFullYear`, so years 0 to 99 stay themselves); the instant is that date plus the hour,
minute and second, minus the zone's offset, so a leap second (60) is the first second of the
next minute. `readMessage` records `invalid-date` for any problem whose words
parsed, and `unparsed-value` for `not a date`.

**Structure** (RFC 2045 5, RFC 2046 5.1.1). A part's header runs from its start to its first
CRLF CRLF (the second CRLF belongs to neither); a part that begins with CRLF has an empty
header; a part with no empty line is all header and an empty body. Content-Type is the first
`content-type` field (unfolded): the type and subtype before `;`, lowercased; parameters as
`name=value` or `name="quoted"`, names lowercased, quoted values with `\` removed, the last of a
repeated name kept. White space may come before the colon (`Content-Type :`). No
Content-Type gives `text/plain`, or `message/rfc822` for a part of `multipart/digest`.

A `message/rfc822` part's body is parsed as a whole message into `message`. A `multipart/*`
part's body is split at delimiter lines: CRLF, `--`, the exact boundary, then optional white
space and CRLF; the CRLF before the dashes belongs to the delimiter. A delimiter at the very
start of the body needs no preceding CRLF (the CRLF that ends the header serves), and one at the
very end of the text needs no CRLF after it. A part's own `multipart-unclosed` is recorded after
its children's problems. Text before the first delimiter (preamble) and after
the closing delimiter (`--boundary--`, epilogue) is no part. Only exact boundary lines count:
`---------- Forwarded message` is body text. No boundary parameter, or an empty
one, records `multipart-no-boundary` and the part has no children; no closing delimiter records
`multipart-unclosed` and the last child runs to the end of the parent's body.

**Depth.** A `multipart/*` or `message/rfc822` part at nesting depth `MAX_DEPTH` (50; the root is
depth 0, each child or encapsulated message one deeper) is read as a leaf: no children, `message`
`null`, and a `too-deep` problem. Real mail nests a few levels; without a bound, a hostile
message thousands of levels deep exhausts the stack, and reading must never throw.

**Part details for IMAP** (RFC 9051 7.5.2). `contentType(value)` is the Content-Type rule above
applied to one field value. `partFields(text, part)` is `headerFields` of the part's header
range, its fields only. `bodyLines(text, part)` is the number of LF in the part's body, plus one
when the body is not empty and does not end in LF; an empty body has 0.

**Sections** (RFC 9051 6.4.5). `section(root, "1.2")` walks the dot-separated numbers from the
root. At each step, a `message/rfc822` part stands for its `message`. A part with children
gives its child `n` (from 1). A part with no children answers only `1`, and only when it is the
root or a message's body; otherwise the section does not exist and the result is `null`.

**Decoding** (RFC 2045 6, RFC 2047). `transferDecode` turns a body's text into bytes; the
encoding name is trimmed and compared without case (RFC 2045 6.1). `base64` ignores characters
outside the alphabet and stops at the first `=`. `quoted-printable` first drops white space at
the end of every line (RFC 2045 6.7 rule 3), then removes soft line breaks (`=` CRLF) and turns
`=XX` (two hex digits, either case) into its byte, leaving any other `=` as it is. Any other
encoding (`7bit`, `8bit`, `binary`, unknown) is the bytes as they are.

`partText` decodes a part's body range with the first Content-Transfer-Encoding field of its
header, unfolded (default `7bit`), then its `charset` parameter (default `us-ascii`) with
`TextDecoder`, not fatal; an unknown charset decodes as `utf-8`.

`decodeWords` replaces each `=?charset?B|Q?text?=` (RFC 2047 2) with its decoded text: `Q`
turns `_` into a space, and the bytes are decoded with `TextDecoder(charset, { fatal: true })`
after an RFC 2231 language suffix (`*en`) is removed from the charset. A word whose charset
`TextDecoder` does not know, or whose bytes are not valid in it, does not decode and is left as
written, as plain text. White space between two words that both decoded is dropped (RFC 2047
6.2); white space next to anything else is kept.

**Reading a message.** `readMessage(bytes)` builds the binary string, takes the header (the
text before the first empty line, lines ending in CRLF, CR or LF; all of it when there is
none), runs `headerFields`, reads From, Sender, Reply-To, To, Cc and
Bcc with `readAddresses`, Message-ID, In-Reply-To and References with `readMessageIds` (a
Message-ID field must hold exactly one), Date with `readDate`, keeps Subject as written (the
first one), runs `parseParts` on the whole message, and returns the header's problems, then the field problems in the order From, Sender,
Message-ID, Date, Reply-To, To, Cc, Bcc, In-Reply-To, References, then the structure's
problems. It never throws.

## Handed on

- **IMAP** formats `ENVELOPE` and `BODYSTRUCTURE` from `Read` and serves `BODY[...]` from
  `section` and the ranges; it decides which deliveries to one mailbox are one message (same
  Message-ID) when it assigns UIDs. Its form owns those rules.
- **RFC 2231** continued and encoded parameters (`filename*0*=`) are read as written for now;
  a later revision decodes them.

## Tests

The writer's harness: every test is a `criterion(id, fn)`, coverage 100%. The requirements are
FR-MAIL-002 to 007 (revised for this form) and FR-MAIL-019 (structure and decoding);
NFR-MAIL-001 to 003 apply to `./read` as to the writer, with NFR-MAIL-001 gaining "the read
part bundled alone includes nothing of another part".

Fixtures: the RFC 5322 Appendix A messages, seven malformed messages and six writer goldens,
kept byte-exact as files under `packages/core/tests/fixtures/read/`, copied from the
`body.fixtures` of research 01a19b44-5fe5-7342-bb90-85b91511d33d. Its `body.goldens.read` is
the `readMessage` of every fixture (dates as ISO strings), checked against Python `email`
(policy compat32); a test compares `readMessage` of each fixture with its golden.

## Not decided

- RFC 2231 parameter decoding (read as written).
- Charsets `TextDecoder` does not know (decoded as UTF-8).
- Reading 8-bit headers as UTF-8 (RFC 6532) beyond the lexer: values are returned as their
  bytes' binary string; a later revision decides.
"
